	// IntrospectAPIKey Resolve a presented API token to its claims
	//
	// Asks "what may this token do, and for which account". It exists because the
	// token is **opaque**: it is `cafaye_` plus 32 random bytes and the row holds
	// a SHA-256 of it, so there is no claim document inside it and no published
	// key set to verify it against. Asking identity is the only way a resource
	// server can learn a scope. The response is RFC 7662's shape.
	//
	// The body takes the **credential, not an id** — an id would be an enumeration
	// oracle over somebody else's credentials, and a value is only useful to
	// somebody who already holds it.
	//
	// ### An unusable token answers `{"active": false}` and nothing else
	//
	// Unknown, revoked, expired, and one whose owner has been removed from the
	// account are all the same 200 with the same body, and the status is 200
	// rather than 404 because the endpoint succeeded — the answer is that the
	// token is not active. A caller who could tell those apart would learn whether
	// a leaked value was live, which is the second question an attacker asks after
	// "does this work".
