// RevokeOIDCClient Revoke an OpenID Connect client
//
// Stops honouring the registration. The row is kept rather than deleted — an
// audit of which credentials existed is worth more than a tidier table, and
// `revoked_at` is what makes the difference readable afterwards.
//
// The revocation is real rather than advisory: every access token issued
// against this registration is revoked in the same transaction, because a
// token is a signed JWT that a verifier on the edge will keep accepting
// until its `exp` and revoking the registration does not by itself stop one.
// A token minted before the revocation therefore fails at userinfo
// immediately, not fifteen minutes later.
//
// 204 rather than the revoked row, matching every other `DELETE` in this
// service. The `identity.oidc_client.revoked` event is where a consumer
