// ### An unusable token answers `{"active": false}` and nothing else
//
// Unknown, revoked, expired, and one whose owner has been removed from the
// account are all the same 200 with the same body, and the status is 200
// rather than 404 because the endpoint succeeded — the answer is that the
// token is not active. A caller who could tell those apart would learn whether
// a leaked value was live, which is the second question an attacker asks after
// "does this work".
//
// ### The caller's own standing is a different question
//
// It gets different statuses, because it is a different fact about the
// request: **401** with no credential, and **403** for a token asking about
// anybody else's. A token may introspect **itself**, which is the case a CI job
// needs when it wants to know why it is being refused. A session may read a
// token in an account it **owns**; a session that is not in the account is
// 404, for the reason every other route does that.
//
// ### The two scope claim names
//
// `scopes` and `scope` are **byte for byte identical**, and both are
// space-separated strings.
//
// This is a deliberate interim, not a contract. The fleet does not agree on the
// name: core's `docs/openapi-conventions.md` requires `scopes`, and guard's
// `src/middleware/jwt.ts` reads `scope` and splits it on whitespace. The
// question is open (MD7 in the manager's `DECISIONS.md`) and answering it
// unilaterally would mean rotating every credential already in a customer's
// hand. Emitting both means whichever name wins, the other is a one-line
// removal and no credential has to be reissued. A space-separated **string**
// rather than an array because guard's verifier refuses a non-string claim.
//
// ### `account_id` is required and there is no `sub` fallback
//
// `sub` is the **user** the token names. `account_id` is the tenancy boundary
// and it is always present; a token with no account cannot be used against any
// account and is answered `{"active": false}`. Nothing here falls back to
// `sub`, which would give a service-to-service token a user as its tenancy
// key and turn a bug in one service into a cross-tenant read.
