// RegisterOIDCClientWithBodyWithResponse Register an OpenID Connect client
//
// Registers a relying party: the credential a product presents at the token
// endpoint when it runs an authorization-code flow against this service.
//
// **Owner only.** Adding a client is adding a new way for code to arrive in
// this product and get a token back out, so a member of the account is 403
// and a non-member is 404.
//
// `client_id` and `client_secret` are minted here and returned here, and
// `client_secret` is returned **once**. It is never stored — only its
// SHA-256 is — and there is no endpoint that re-reads it. A caller that
// loses it registers again, which is the rule a session token, an
// invitation token and a password already follow.
//
// `redirect_uris` is matched EXACTLY. No prefix, no glob, no pattern of
// any kind: `https://app.example.com.evil.com/cb` is not a match for a
// registered `https://app.example.com/cb`, and a prefix comparison would
// make this service an open redirector for every product registered here.
// Plain `http` is accepted only on loopback, so a product can develop
// against a local stack without the registration ever accepting a
// plain-http origin.
//
// `scopes` is intersected with what this provider implements
// (`openid email profile accounts`) and must include `openid`. `grant_types`
// must be `authorization_code`: this service issues no refresh token, has
// no service identity for client credentials, and has no consent screen for
// an implicit flow, so registering any of them is refused at 422 with the
// field named rather than discovered at 3am.
//
// Takes any type of body and a specified content type, and returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with POST /v1/accounts/{account_id}/oidc-clients (the `RegisterOIDCClient` operationId).
